#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# eval

#% text
# `eval(e)` gives the value of `e`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Outside a held argument `eval` changes nothing: its argument is evaluated like any other, and `eval(e)` is that value.

#% item
# Inside an argument a function holds (the integrand of `plot`, the body of `table`), `eval(e)` marks `e` to be evaluated when the function evaluates that part.

#% item
# On the right of a definition, `eval` is the identity too: `rule g(x_) := eval(x^2 + 1)` behaves as `rule g(x_) := x^2 + 1`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
eval(1 + 1)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
rule g(x_) := eval(x^2 + 1)
g(3)
#% output
#= 10

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Inside a held argument: `quote` keeps `1 + 1`, `eval` inside it is reached when the quoted term is released:

#% cell
unquote(quote(eval(1 + 1)))
#% output
#= unquote(quote(2))

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `eval` of a value is that value:

#% cell
eval([1, x, "s"]) === [1, x, "s"]
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `quote`, `unquote`
